--- note
## Why this is in our learning hub (honest framing)
This lesson set is **not about biohub cell-tracking**. It walks the *Gemma 4 Technical Report*
(Google DeepMind, arXiv 2607.02770, 2026-06-19), an open-weight **large language model** suite.
It lives here because our fleet is driven by LLM agents (the leader / researcher / trainer), and
because Gemma 4's engineering — MTP speculative decoding, KV-cache sharing, QAT, MoE routing — is
exactly the **LLM/agent-infra** that Part B of this effort turns into reusable fleet agents. Read it
as *infra background*, not as a cell-tracking method.

--- note
## What Gemma 4 is
Gemma 4 is a new generation of **open-weight, natively multimodal** language models released under an
**Apache 2.0** license. "Natively multimodal" means one model seamlessly processes **text, images and
audio** (audio for E2B, E4B and 12B). The suite is designed to run across a range of **on-device
hardware**, and the report frames its two headline goals as **compute efficiency** and **reasoning**.

The family spans a wide size range and two architecture styles:

- **Dense** models: effective **2.3B (E2B)**, **4.5B (E4B)**, **12B**, and **31B** parameters.
- **Mixture-of-Experts (MoE)**: **26B-A4B** — ~**3.8B activated** parameters out of **26B total**
  (only a few experts fire per token, so you pay ~4B of compute for 26B of capacity).

E2B and E4B use **per-layer embeddings** (as in Gemma 3n), which is why they are *2.3B / 4.5B
effective* out of *5B / 8B total* — most of the extra weight is cheap embedding lookup, not compute.

